==Classification==

The Astrocystitidae, which consisted only of ''Astrocystites ottawaensis'' until 1968,<ref>{{harvnb|Webby|1968}}</ref> were initially classified as [[cystoid]]s, a group that at that time included all stalked echinoderms that were neither [[crinoids]] nor [[blastoids]].  It was subsequently placed by different authors with either the blastoids or (somewhat more frequently) the edrioasteroids, although a resemblence to the [[parablastoids]] was also noted.<ref>{{harvnb|Fay|1967|pp=S292</ref> Despite the trend towards considering ''Astrocystites'' to be an edrioasteroid, the class Edrioblastoidea was erected by Robert O. Fay to contain the family in 1962.<ref>{{harvnb|Fay|1967|pp=S290}}</ref>  This arrangement persisted until 1990.

===External Taxonomy===

Three placements of Aristocystitidae have been proposed since the 1990s, differing only on the rank of the parent edrioblastoid taxon, and on the presence and rank of additional taxa under that parent.

In 1990, Smith and Jell demoted class Edrioblastoidea to an order of Edrioasteroidea without changing its contents, which consisted of only the Aristocystitidae:<ref>{{harvnb|Smith|Jell|1990}}</ref>

* Class Edrioasteroidea {{small|Billings 1858}}
** Order Edrioblastoida {{small|Fay 1952 (nomen transl.)}}
*** Family Aristocystitidae {{small|Bassler 1935}}

In 1994, Guensburg and Sprinkle acted on the long-observed affinities of edrioblastoids the Edrioasterida,<ref>{{harvnb|Webby|1968|p=515}}</ref> and even closer affinity with cyathocystids<ref>{{harvnb|Bell|1980|p=168}}</ref> and rhenopyrgids:<ref>{{harvnb|Guensburg|Sprinkle|1994|pp=13, 20–21}}</ref>

* Class Edrioasteroidea {{small|Billings 1858}}
** Order Edrioasterida {{small|Bell 1976 (emend.)}}
*** Suborder Edrioblastoidina {{small|Fay 1952 (nomen transl., emend.)}}
**** Family Aristocystitidae {{small|Bassler 1935}}
**** Family Cyathocystidae {{small|Bather 1899 (emend.)}}
***** Subfamily Cyathocystinae {{small|Bather 1899 (nomen transl., emend.)}}
***** Subfamily Rhenopyrginae {{small|Holloway and Jell 1983 (nomen transl., emend.)}}

In a 2014 paper focusing on rhenopyrgids, Sumrall ''et al.'' noted that neitehr Guensburg and Sprinkle's phylogenetic analsys nor their own demonstrated a closer relationship between rhenopyrgids and cyathocystids than between either of them and astrocystitids, and promoted rhenopyrgids back to the family level:<ref>{{harvnb|Sumrall|Herida|Rodríguez|Mestre|2013|p=773}}</ref>

* Class Edrioasteroidea {{small|Billings 1858}}
** Order Edrioasterida {{small|Bell 1976}}
*** Suborder Edrioblastoidina {{small|Fay 1952}}
**** Family Aristocystitidae {{small|Bassler 1935}}
**** Family Cyathocystidae {{small|Bather 1899}}
**** Family Rhenopyrgidae {{small|Holloway and Jell 1983 (emend.)}}

===Equivalence with edrioblastoids ''sensu stricto''===

Traditionally, the Astrocystitidae have been the only edrioblastoid family. Authors who treat edrioblastoids as an order per Smith and Jell above have recently omitted the family level classification, despite its well-established use since the late 1800s.<ref>{{harvnb|Zhu|Zamora|Lefebvre|2014|p=991}}</ref><ref name=JS21p27 />

Even among authors that use the expanded suborder, the term "edrioblastoid" is often used informally to refer only to astrocystitids, as opposed to cyathocystids and/or rhenopyrgids.<ref>{{harvnb|Sprinkle|Sumrall|p=346}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Sumrall|Herida|Rodríguez|Mestre|2013|p=766}}</ref>

===Astrocystitidae vs Steganoblastidae===

Soon after ''Astrocystites'' was named by Whiteaves in 1897, [[Francis Arthur Bather]] objected that it was too likely to be confused with ''Asterocystis'' (Haeckel) and suggested ''Steganoblastus'' as an alternative.  Whiteaves complied in 1898, after which Bather erected the family Steganoblastidae within the Edrioasteroidea in 1900.  [[Ray S. Bassler]] restored ''Astrocystites'' on grounds of [[priority (biology)|priority]] in 1935, and erected the family Astrocystitidae to match it.  Some have argued that while ''Steganoblastus'' is a junior synonym of ''Astrocystites'', Steganoblastidae has priority over Astrocystitidae and was widely used, qualifying it for retention under article 40 of the [[International Code of Zoological Nomenclature]].<ref>{{harvnb|Webby|1968|pp=513–514}}</ref>  However, as seen from citations on this page, Astrocytitidae has been more commonly used in recent decades.

===Internal Phylogeny===

No comprehensive cladistic analysis of the Astrocystitidae has yet been published.  However, it has been noted that the later genera do appear to be more derived than the earlier ones, and that the early Ordovician ''Porosublastus'' and ''Lampteroblastus'' seem more closely related to each other than to the more basal late Cambrian ''Cambroblastus'' or the more derived late Ordovician ''Astrocystites''.<ref>{{harvnb|Sprinkle|Sumrall|2015|p=351}}</ref>  If the middle Cambrian ''Ikerus'' is an astrocystitid, it appears to be even more basal due to the presence of epispires, a [[plesiomorphic]] feature present in early edrioasteroids and eocrinoids.<ref>{{harvnb|Jell|Sprinkle|2021|p=33}}</ref>


