===Equivalence with edrioblastoids ''sensu stricto''===

Traditionally, the Astrocystitidae have been the only edrioblastoid family, including after the class's demotion to the edrioasteroid order Edrioblastoida in 1990 by Smith and Jell.<ref>{{harvnb|Smith|Jell|1990|p=742}}</ref>

More recently, edrioblastoids were further demoted by Guensburg and Sprinkle to the suborder Edrioblastoidina within the order Edrioasterida, and expanded to include the cyathocystids and rhenopyrgids as either one<ref>{{harvnb|Guensburg|Sprinkle|1994|pp=20–21}}</ref> or two<ref>{{harvnb|Sumrall|Herida|Rodríguez|Mestre|2013|p=773}}</ref> additional families.  A possible relationship among these groups had been noted since at least 1980.<ref>{{harvnb|Bell|1980|p=168}}</ref> Even before that, similarities had been notied between ''Astrocystites'' and ''Edrioaster'', presaging the grouping of Edrioblastoidina and [[Edrioasterina]].<ref>{{harvnb|Webby|1968|p=515}}</ref>


at the time the order Edrioblastoida was established.<ref>{{harvnb|Smith|Jell|1990|pp=744–745, 750}}</ref>  

Even among authors that use the expanded suborder, the term "edrioblastoid" is often used informally to refer only to astrocystitids, as opposed to cyathocystids and/or rhenopyrgids.<ref>{{harvnb|Sprinkle|Sumrall|p=346}}</ref><ref>{{harvnb|Sumrall|Herida|Rodríguez|Mestre|2013|p=766}}</ref>  Authors who treat edrioblastoids as an order have recently omitted the family level classification, despite its well-established use since the late 1800s.<ref>{{harvnb|Zhu|Zamora|Lefebvre|2014|p=991}}</ref><ref name=JS21p27 />
